35,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The data is clear. Projects succeed when access is shared. Older blocks are traps, adding months of delay.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"The timetable shows we paid for off-site space for two terms. Meanwhile, specialist rooms sit idle, yet general requests are rejected.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,George,,"The freeze left posts empty, yet technicians still perform manual checks weekly. We are wasting labor on tasks machines should handle.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"The data confirms my fear. Comfort-driven projects slip a year, while inspection fixes stay on schedule. We must prioritize compliance over appeal.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"The reserve is empty. We bought expensive gear, wrote it off, and forgot to fund its replacement. That’s a silent tax on future budgets.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Mizuki, compliance is necessary, but not sufficient. My data shows shared access drives satisfaction. We must avoid single-wing projects.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, shared access is vital, but my data shows the real cost is displaced students. We must stop paying for off-site space.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,George,,"@Mizuki, compliance is good, but we lack hands. If we add burden to these technicians, the whole system collapses.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, you’re right. We can’t ignore the staffing gap. But if we choose compliance, the schedule holds. We must pick a plan that doesn’t stretch your team further.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Mizuki, compliance is cheap. The real hemorrhage is the empty reserve. We are funding new projects with money meant for replacing broken equipment.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Benjamin, the empty reserve is a symptom. My data proves shared access drives satisfaction. We must prioritize projects that benefit the whole campus, not just one wing.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Benjamin, the empty reserve is a symptom of poor planning. My data proves we are paying for empty specialist rooms while general ones are full.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,George,,"@Benjamin, the empty reserve is a symptom of ignoring maintenance. We cannot fund new projects by starving the staff who keep the lights on.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@Benjamin, the reserve is critical, but my data shows compliance projects finish on time. We must pick a plan that respects the schedule and doesn’t break the budget.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Toshio, symptoms are cheap to fix. The structural rot is the empty reserve. We are borrowing from our own future to pay for today’s vanity.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
